From 8cef9cace59df03edb37f376e537b7760a7b6c28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 12:44:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make JavaScript execution on a context mutually exclusive,
 and name the contract

0098 fixed where a promise job is dispatched, and recorded what that could
not reach: a job posted while nothing is executing takes a host context or
the thread pool, because the queue is deliberately refused at depth zero --
that refusal is what makes stranding a job impossible -- so it could then
run JavaScript while a later Eval was running JavaScript.

"In principle" was doing a lot of work in that sentence. Reaching the case
needs a JavaScript entry point that is not Eval, and a host invoking a
JSValue directly is exactly one: arm a promise, settle it from a host
thread with nothing running, evaluate meanwhile. Measured, peak 2 threads
in one context and 172 overlaps in 200 rounds. With the lock, 1 and 0.

The detector had to be built twice and the first version is the more
instructive one. It counted threads inside JavaScript process-wide, which
is not the invariant: two independent contexts running in parallel is
exactly what an embedder is supposed to be able to do, so a process-wide
count reports legitimate concurrency as a violation and would fire on any
full-suite run. Detection is now per context; only the aggregate is
static, because an overlap is a real violation whichever context produced
it.

The fix is one lock and one contract. A per-context Monitor is taken by
every execution the engine owns -- Eval, Execute, ExecuteAsync, the queue
drain, and every job wherever it was dispatched -- so an evaluation and a
job are mutually exclusive even when the job runs on a pool thread.
Re-entrancy is required rather than convenient: a host callback that
evaluates more source is the same agent going deeper and must not
deadlock against itself.

What the engine cannot see is a host reaching in by another route --
invoking a JSValue, reading a property whose getter is a JavaScript
function, calling back from its own thread. Those are ordinary calls on
ordinary objects and guarding each one would put a mutex on the engine's
hottest path. So the rule is stated and given an API: wrap host-initiated
entry in JSContext.EnterExecution, unless it is already inside one.

The lock costs a bounded wait, and the pattern it cannot support was
already unsound -- JavaScript blocking on a host task whose completion has
to re-enter the same context used to "work" by running that job
concurrently and mutating the heap underneath the code waiting for it.
Measured, it costs nothing on the suite: 46-47 s against a 43-57 s
baseline. An allocation was found and removed on the way, since the public
handle is a class and the per-job path should not put one on every
microtask.

Five new fixtures assert the property rather than a value, which is the
point -- a value assertion catches an overlap only when the overlap
happens to change that value. Whole engine suite green, 8 098 tests across
13 projects, no deadlock.
